  "title": "Microsoft Azure architecture identity networking compute and data services",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "Azure resources are governed through a hierarchy of management groups, subscriptions, resource groups, and resources. Azure Resource Manager (ARM) is the control plane for deployment and management; it is separate from service data-plane operations.",
    "Microsoft Entra ID represents identities and applications. An app registration describes an application identity, a service principal is its tenant-local representation, and managed identities provide Azure-hosted workloads credentials without storing application secrets.",
    "Azure RBAC role assignments bind a security principal, role definition, and scope. Scope inheritance and deny assignments matter; authentication to Entra does not by itself grant a resource permission.",
    "An Azure region can have different architectures and availability-zone support. Zonal, zone-redundant, and multi-region deployments have different reliability, data-residency, cost, and operational consequences.",
    "Azure networking composes VNets, subnets, routes, network security groups, DNS, gateways, and private connectivity. A private endpoint changes access to a service but DNS and routing must still resolve and reach the intended private address.",
    "Compute and data services trade control for operational responsibility. A VM exposes operating-system administration; App Service, Container Apps, Functions, AKS, and managed databases expose different application and platform boundaries."
  ],
